WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.840
Cinema history is full of dazzling technical
achievements and masterful editing, but some

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:09.380
of the most impressive sequences of all time
are shot in one long, fluid take.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.420
Actors and crew members practice the elaborate
sequence until it's second nature, rehearsing

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:17.779
every line and camera movement until they
are able to execute a gorgeous sequence in

00:00:17.779 --> 00:00:19.680
a single unbroken shot.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.480
Here are a few of the best examples of the
form.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.659
Goodfellas

00:00:23.659 --> 00:00:28.699
The films of Martin Scorsese are absolutely
loaded with iconic shots, but few are as well-remembered

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:31.589
as this gorgeous long take from Goodfellas.

00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:36.090
Known as "The Copa Shot," it's a nearly three-minute
Steadicam take that follows Henry and Karen

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:40.210
as they enter the Copacabana nightclub through
a side entrance so Henry can flaunt his many

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:41.210
mob-based connections.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:45.780
According to camera operator Larry McConkey,
the shot was originally just a simple case

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.730
of the camera following the actors, but he
and cinematographer Michael Ballhaus decided

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.329
to spice up the blocking.

00:00:51.329 --> 00:00:55.320
They did this in part because McConkey needed
pauses in the walk so he could transition

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.480
between wide shots and close-ups without cutting
away.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.499
That's why they added touches like a walk
through the club's kitchen and the moment

00:01:01.499 --> 00:01:04.350
when Henry stops to playfully chastise a flirting
couple.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.660
"Every time I come here.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.520
Every time, you two!"

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:10.910
Creed

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.950
Nearly 40 years after the first Rocky film,
the franchise revitalizer Creed delivered

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.500
a dazzling, uninterrupted fight sequence.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.960
For the fight between Adonis and Leo "The
Lion" Sporino, director Ryan Coogler wanted

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.060
to achieve the visual metaphor of the two
boxers being suddenly all on their own after

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.230
months of working with their trainers.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:32.270
So the fight was filmed as one continuous
Steadicam take, with the camera dancing around

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:33.480
the actors.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:37.840
Coogler shot 13 takes of the fight, with no
additional cameras on set to provide extra

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.150
coverage should he later decide to break it
up in the editing room.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:45.340
The result is an intense, intimate sequence
that ranks among the best moments in boxing

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.950
cinema since Scorsese's Raging Bull.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.450
Children of Men

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:54.440
The acclaimed four minute car attack scene
in 2006's Children of Men was shot with a

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:58.420
special rig that not only allowed the camera
to move around within the cramped car, but

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.080
move out of it later.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.820
There are a lot of moving parts in this scene.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.600
To make things even more stressful, director
Alfonso Cuarón revealed during a 2013 San

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.380
Diego Comic-Con panel that he only had approximately
four takes available to get the shot right.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:16.590
It ended up coming down to the very last take
before the production was going to lose access

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.670
to the location, and Cuarón almost ruined
that one himself.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:23.610
"We were shooting the last take, everything
goes great, but then by accident the blood

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:24.610
spills onto the lens.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:28.380
I yelled cut, but there was an explosion and
nobody heard me so they kept shooting.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.850
Then, later, I realized that the blood splash
was the miracle."

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:36.700
The final take, the one Cuarón almost abandoned
mid-shot, is the one in the film.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:37.720
The Player

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.819
Robert Altman was one of the most famously
playful directors of the 20th century.

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:45.930
The Player, a dark satire about a ruthless
Hollywood executive, is essentially a movie

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:50.510
about movies, so Altman took every chance
possible to cram in references to classic

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:51.510
films.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.370
He also took the opportunity to give us the
entire look and feel of the film in an unbroken,

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.160
eight-minute opening shot.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:00.970
Beginning with the tongue-in-cheek clicking
of a film slate, the take pulls back and rises

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:04.840
up into a wide crane shot in an homage to
Orson Welles' Touch of Evil.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.920
Then the camera dances around the lot, following
various characters and nodding to several

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.630
cinema classics.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:14.010
Even if you're a true movie buff, it'll take
you a few tries to catch every reference and

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.740
inside joke in the uncut sequence.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:16.740
"Oh, I see.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:21.220
It's kind of like a Gods Must Be Crazy except
the Coke bottle is now a television actress."

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:22.220
"Yeah, that's exactly right.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.510
It's Out of Africa meets Pretty Woman."

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:24.990
The Protector

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:29.410
Though the final product often looks amazing,
shooting heavily edited fight scenes like

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:33.390
this Taken 3 sequence can be tedious work,
with tons of close-ups of hands, feet, and

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:34.410
faces.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:39.660
Or, you can do what the creators of 2005's
Thai action classic The Protector did: shoot

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:44.330
a badass, four-minute fight scene featuring
one hero, one camera, dozens of stuntmen,

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:47.550
and lots of stairs, in a single amazing take.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:52.030
In this sequence, the hero has made his way
to a restaurant specializing in exotic meats

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:56.060
where, he believes, a gangster is holding
his beloved elephants.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:00.530
Proving just how far one man will go for his
animal friends, Kham storms the restaurant's

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:05.970
VIP area, where he's confronted by an enormous
spiral staircase and a load of henchmen ready

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:06.989
to take him out.

00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:10.160
The result is one of the best fight sequences
of the past two decades.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.660
Plus, it has arguably the best action hero
closing line in the history of cinema.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:17.979
Thanks for watching!

00:04:17.979 --> 00:04:20.079
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:22.139
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

